course: dictionary
lesson: 14770
title: Protectorate: protected, but not fully ruled
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A protectorate is a country or area that a stronger country controls and protects, while it keeps some of its own rulers. Look at the word itself: protect means to keep safe, so a protectorate is a place that someone powerful guards. «The small island became a protectorate after the big country promised to guard it.» The clue is guard, so the island is being looked after by someone else.
explain: The island still had its own rulers, so it was not simply taken over. «The island kept its own chief, but the stronger country decided how its money and its laws would be handled.» Try your meaning in the sentence: "a place controlled and protected by a stronger country" fits perfectly, so you have the right meaning.
explain: In history lessons this word comes up with empires. «The British protectorate» and «become a protectorate» are the phrases to look for. When you see them, ask: which country is in charge, and which country is being protected?
fact: A protectorate is a country or area controlled and protected by a stronger country.
fact: A protectorate keeps some of its own rulers, so it is not fully part of the stronger country.
fact: Protectorate can also mean the relationship of protection and control between the two countries.
fact: The Protectorate, with a capital P, was the time from 1653 to 1659, when Oliver Cromwell and then his son ruled England, Scotland and Ireland as «Lord Protector».
fact: Britain, France and other powers ran protectorates in Africa, Asia and the Pacific.
exercise: «The small island became a protectorate after the big country promised to guard it.» What does protectorate mean here? | A busy port full of ships | A country that rules all others | A school for young explorers | *A place controlled and protected by a stronger country
exercise: «The island kept its own chief, but a stronger country guarded its shores.» Which word describes this island? | Colony of the sea | Capital | Empire | *Protectorate
exercise: When was the Protectorate in England? | 1066 to 1087 | *1653 to 1659 | 1914 to 1918 | 1800 to 1820
test: «The island became a British protectorate.» Does a protectorate keep any of its own rulers? | No, it has no rulers at all | No, it is always the largest country in the region | *Yes, it keeps some of its own rulers
test: What does «Lord Protector» refer to in the Protectorate? | A ship captain in the Pacific | The king of France | *Oliver Cromwell, who ruled England, Scotland and Ireland
exam: Which of these best describes a protectorate? | *A country controlled and protected by a stronger country, while keeping some of its own rulers | A country that fights every neighbour | A powerful empire with no rivals | A ship used to guard harbours
